<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#muharram">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muharram</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muharram is the first month of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamic-calendar">Islamic lunar calendar</a> and one of its four sacred months. Its tenth day, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashura">Ashura</a>, marks the death of the Prophet's grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husayn-ibn-ali">Husayn ibn Ali</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-karbala">Battle of Karbala</a> in 680. Shia Muslims commemorate it with mourning gatherings, processions and passion plays, while many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunnis</a> observe Ashura with fasting.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muharram.jpg" alt="Muharram" width="320" height="194" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Abbas Shariati · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ashura_2016_mourning_in_Imam_Hossein_Square,_Tehran_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Muharram, meaning sacred, is the first month of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamic-calendar">Islamic calendar</a>, with at most thirty days. Warfare in it was forbidden before the advent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. The word is short for Muharram Safar, the sacred Safar, which in the ancient Arab calendar distinguished Safar I, which was sacred, from Safar II, which was not. Over time the adjective itself became the name of the first month of the year.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamic-calendar">Islamic calendar</a> is lunar, and months begin when the first crescent of a new moon is sighted. Because the lunar year is eleven or twelve days shorter than the solar year, Muharram falls on different days in successive solar years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The tenth day of Muharram is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashura">Ashura</a>. For Sunni Muslims it marks the parting of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a> and the salvation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a>, and tradition also places on this day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/noah">Noah</a>'s disembarking from the Ark, God's forgiveness of Adam and the release of Joseph from prison. It is observed by voluntary fasting and other pious acts. Some Sunni communities hold carnivals, bonfires and special dishes, though some Sunni scholars have criticised such practices.</p><p>For Shia Muslims Ashura is a day of mourning for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husayn-ibn-ali">Husayn ibn Ali</a>, grandson of Muhammad and the third <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a>. Husayn refused on moral grounds to pledge allegiance to the Umayyad caliph Yazid ibn Mu'awiya and was killed, with most of his male relatives and his small retinue, by the Umayyad army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-karbala">Battle of Karbala</a> on Ashura in 61 AH (680 CE). <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunnis</a> also regard the killing of Husayn as a tragic event, and Husayn and his companions are widely regarded as martyrs by all Muslims.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The events of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-karbala">Karbala</a> are remembered day by day. Husayn arrived there on 2 Muharram and was forced to camp in the desert; on 7 Muharram the Umayyad army cut off his access to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>; on 9 Muharram, called Tasu'a, negotiations failed and the commander Umar ibn Sa'd was persuaded to delay the attack until the next day. After the battle the women and children of the camp were taken prisoner and marched to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>.</p><p>Most Shia mourning takes place in the first ten days, ending with processions in major Shia cities on the tenth. Practices include gatherings in mosques and husayniyyas where scholars and maddahs recount <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-karbala">Karbala</a>, the recitation of elegies, charitable distribution of food and water, reenactments, and ritual chest-beating, called latmiyya or sīnah-zanī. Among the Shia, mourning for Husayn is viewed as protest against oppression and as a struggle for God.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Muharram” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The first day of the year of the Hijra, 1 <b>Muharram</b> AH 1, corresponds to 16 July 622.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-16">16 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The shrine of Hussain Tekri, built by a Nawab in the 19th century, draws pilgrims during <b>Muharram</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/jaora-state">Jaora State</a></li></ul></section>
